<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mytilene</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 BCE – 79 BCE (approx.) · capital Mytilene</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Chief city of Lesbos, home of the poets Sappho and Alcaeus, whose revolt against Athens in 428 BCE prompted the famous Mytilenean Debate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeolians">Aeolian Greeks</a> settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a> around 1000 BCE, and Mytilene grew on the south-east coast of the island, with harbours facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. By the 7th century BCE it was the leading city of Lesbos, founding settlements in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troad">Troad</a> and fighting <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigeion">Sigeion</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Aristocratic feuds and tyrants such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrsilus">Myrsilus</a> marked the 7th century BCE, ended when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pittacus">Pittacus</a>, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-sages-of-greece">Seven Sages</a>, ruled as elected aesymnetes c. 590 to 580 BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sappho">Sappho</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcaeus">Alcaeus</a> wrote lyric poetry there. Mytilene shared in the Greek trading post at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naucratis</a>, and later belonged to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a> as a ship-providing ally.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After its revolt of 428 BCE <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> first voted to kill all its men, then relented. Mytilene sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus</a>, and its capture by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 79 BCE, where the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> won the civic crown, ended its independence, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a> later granted it freedom.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mytilene.jpg" alt="Mytilene" width="320" height="477"><figcaption>Image: SilentResident (compilation, sources to attribute see above; if any of the sources used in the Montage are removed fr... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mytilene_Montage_L.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 BCE – 79 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century-bce">11th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mytilene</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pittacus, Myrsilus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Aeolic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Mitylene</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-540&amp;focus=mytilene" aria-label="Mytilene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5337 776 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mytilene lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5657 1032L6140 1251M5657 1032L5591 1068M5657 1032L5591 1068M5657 1032L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5591 1068h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5657 1032h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5657 1032h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-540&amp;focus=mytilene">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

